      "mathSignatureCandidate": [
        "4\\pi",
        "I=\\frac{\\Phi}{4\\pi}"
      ],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "对于向所有方向均匀发光的理想点光源，全部方向的立体角为 $4\\pi$ 球面度，因此：\n$I=\\frac{\\Phi}{4\\pi}$ (6.4)",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": false,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "zh",
      "ordinal": 16,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0176#1"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [
        "\\Phi"
      ],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [
        "\\Phi"
      ],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "这里的 $\\Phi$ 是光源向所有方向发出的总光通量，即按视觉响应评价的总光辐射功率。",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": false,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "zh",
      "ordinal": 17,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0176#2"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "国际单位制采用坎德拉作为发光强度的单位①。\n原书用专门的发光器说明发光强度单位的度量标准，例如 1 坎德拉；\n这种说明不意味着测量标准可以任意选取。\n\n光通量等其他光度学量的单位，可以由发光强度单位导出。",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "zh",
      "ordinal": 18,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0176#9"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "① 原书脚注记录了“烛光”这个旧名称；\n本课采用坎德拉名称。",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "zh",
      "ordinal": 19,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0176#3"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "光通量的单位。\n光通量的单位是流明。\n发光强度为 1 坎德拉且在所考虑方向范围内均匀发光的理想点光源，在 1 球面度立体角内发出的光通量为 1 流明。",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 1,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0173#11"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "Light can act on an object because it carries energy.\nMaxwell's electromagnetic theory describes light as an electromagnetic wave, so light energy is electromagnetic-wave energy.\n\nPhotometry studies light measurements related to visual response.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 2,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0173#12",
        "p0174#1"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [
        "\\gamma"
      ],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [
        "\\gamma"
      ],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "Different physical quantities describe the transfer of light energy, including luminous flux.\n\nPhotometry concerns the part perceptible to the human eye and evaluates optical radiation according to visual response.\n\nRadio waves have very long wavelengths, while $\\gamma$ rays have very short wavelengths;\nneither is directly visible to the human eye.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 3,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0174#2"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [
        "\\Phi"
      ],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [
        "\\Phi"
      ],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "Luminous flux. Under ordinary photopic viewing conditions, the human eye is relatively sensitive to green light and does not respond equally to every wavelength.\n\nA measurement of radiant energy alone therefore does not describe its visual effect;\nthat energy must also be evaluated according to visual response.\n\nThe resulting quantity is luminous flux, denoted by $\\Phi$.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 4,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0174#3"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "Luminous flux is the optical-radiation energy passing through a surface per unit time, evaluated according to visual response.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": false,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 5,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0174#4"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "In other words, luminous flux is optical-radiation power weighted according to visual response, not radiant power treated indiscriminately as visual effect.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": false,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 6,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0174#5"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "A light source produces luminous flux, which reaches the surfaces of surrounding objects.\n\nThis process also requires luminous intensity, which characterizes the source, and illuminance, which characterizes the receiving surface.\n\nWe begin with the luminous intensity of an ideal point source.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 7,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0174#6"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "Point source. A point-source approximation is appropriate when the source dimensions are much smaller than its distance from the position under study.\n\nThis lesson makes a separate idealizing assumption that the source emits uniformly in every direction;\nthe point-source approximation alone does not guarantee that property.\n\nA distant star can illustrate the point-source approximation even though the star itself has a large volume.\n\nLike a particle, a perfectly rigid body, or an ideal gas, this model retains the important properties for the study and neglects secondary details.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": true,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
      "ordinal": 8,
      "sourceRefs": [
        "p0174#7",
        "p0175#1"
      ],
      "nativeTextErrors": [],
      "delimiterErrors": [],
      "mathSignatureSource": [],
      "mathSignatureCandidate": [],
      "inMemoryNormalizedText": "Solid angle. A solid angle describes the directional region into which a source emits luminous flux.",
      "normalizationWouldChangeText": false,
      "sourceSnapshotPreserved": true,
      "sourceRefsPreserved": true,
      "embeddedSourcePreserved": true,
      "textMatchesAuthoredList": true
    },
    {
      "language": "en",
